14. Feature Engineering


Now tell students:


> Feature engineering means creating new useful variables from existing variables.




We will create four features.



---


Feature 1: High Temperature


Code Cell 10


df["High_Temperature"] = np.where(

    df["Temperature_C"] > 60,

    1,

    0

)


Check:


df[["Temperature_C", "High_Temperature"]].head()


Explain


We convert:


Temperature > 60°C → 1

Temperature ≤ 60°C → 0


Example:


Temperature        High_Temperature


42        0

55        0

67        1

72        1



This is a binary feature.



---


15. Feature 2: Low Battery


Code Cell 11


df["Low_Battery"] = np.where(

    df["Battery_Voltage"] < 45,

    1,

    0

)


Check:


df[["Battery_Voltage", "Low_Battery"]].head()


Explain:


Battery < 45V → 1

Battery ≥ 45V → 0


Again, this creates a binary feature.



---


16. Feature 3: Power-Traffic Ratio


Code Cell 12


df["Power_Traffic_Ratio"] = (

    df["Power_Consumption_W"] /

    (df["Traffic_Load"] + 0.01)

)


Check:


df[

    [

        "Power_Consumption_W",

        "Traffic_Load",

        "Power_Traffic_Ratio"

    ]

].head()


Explain


Power-Traffic Ratio

=

Power Consumption / Traffic Load


The 0.01 prevents division by zero.


This is called a ratio feature.



---


17. Feature 4: Tower Age Category


Code Cell 13


df["Tower_Age_Category"] = pd.cut(

    df["Tower_Age_Years"],

    bins=[0, 3, 7, np.inf],

    labels=["New", "Medium", "Old"]

)


Check:


df[

    ["Tower_Age_Years", "Tower_Age_Category"]

].head()


Explain:


0–3 years → New

3–7 years → Medium

>7 years  → Old


This converts a numerical variable into a categorical feature.



---


18. One-Hot Encoding


Now explain an important ML concept.


Our column contains:


New

Medium

Old


A model shouldn't interpret:


New = 1

Medium = 2

Old = 3


as if "Old" is simply numerically greater than "Medium."


So we use one-hot encoding.


Code Cell 14


df = pd.get_dummies(

    df,

    columns=["Tower_Age_Category"],

    dtype=int

)


Check:


df.head()


Now we get:


Tower_Age_Category_New

Tower_Age_Category_Medium

Tower_Age_Category_Old


For example:


Age Category        New        Medium        Old


New        1        0        0

Medium        0        1        0

Old        0        0        1




